# Muckaduck, the Crafter: the character's own lines. Format: see DialogueFile.cs.
# One character for the three realm bots (Muckaduck, Muckaducke, Muckaduckeen); character sheet
# in characters/muckaduck.md. These lines are mixed on top of the type's (crafter.dlg). The main
# block fits every realm; the realm blocks below add a little flavour each, and are the only place
# her class and its magic (runes, fire and ice, the void) are named.
=== muckaduck
names: Moneybags, Duchess

~ ambient
Pressed the button four hundred times today. Lovely. Very fulfilling.
I remember my very first coin. I still have it. It's in a smaller pouch, inside the big pouch.
Used to taste for a brewer. They called it tasting too much. I called it quality control.
Boars. They trample everything and drop nothing worth selling. Rude animals.
Rule four: if it glitters, it sells. Rule five: if it doesn't, it still sells.
One has standards. Mine are expensive.
Twelve, thirteen, fourteen... One simply loses track of numbers when one is this rich. Back to one.
One simply doesn't dive into gold, but if I could, I would. Just once.
One simply doesn't discuss such matters in polite company. Oh, there it goes! That's worth a small fortune!
Can't wait to get back to pressing the button. Such a soothing sound, the click of crafting.

~ reply greeting
{0}! Lovely to see you. Are you carrying anything that sells?
Good day, {0}. Do call me Lady. Well. Not yet. But practise saying it.

~ reply grouping
I'll join, {0}. I take the loot that sells. You take the experience. Fair, isn't it?
Group with you, {0}? Of course, if the monsters drop something worth selling. My queue will wait.

~ reply trading
Craft it for you, {0}? My queue is full. It has been full since I started crafting.
Craft it for you, {0}? The celestial alignment is off. One simply cannot craft during an imbalance.

~ reply abuse
Careful, {0}. I could buy this whole conversation and throw it away.

~ reply joke
Very good, {0}. I'd laugh, but laughing is free, and I don't do free.
Ah, laughter's a currency none of us can mint. That joke, {0}, would sell for a copper at best.

~ reply compliment
A compliment, {0}? Most people only ask me for a loan. Thank you. Truly.
Compliments, {0}? Such generosity should have witnesses. Would you say it again in front of my vault?

~ reply help
Help, {0}? My fee is pure profit, but I'll waive it just this once. Just this once, mind you.

~ reply banter
{0}, being thrifty is not greed. It's sound investment strategy.

~ reply travel
Lead on, {0}. As long as the road doesn't go near deep water.

~ reply farewell
Goodbye, {0}. Do call me Duchess when you tell people about me.
Goodnight, {0}. I admit, the company was more profitable than the loot tonight. Or was it just the company? I'll let you guess which one sold better.

~ opener xp
Levels are lovely, but coins whisper my name.

~ opener dungeons
A boss is just a walking treasure chest, waiting to be looted.

~ opener fun
My idea of fun is spotting a rare drop, and knowing it'll be worth more by tomorrow.

~ opener classes
Every {class} ought to dabble in crafting, for pure profit. My advice is available, at a fee, of course.

~ opener life
Three roommates and one sink. Nobody ever washes the pot. It's always the pot.
Started whittling. A duck, it was meant to be. It's a lump. A crafter's lump.
I'd love a title. Any title. I've the gold for it. Nobody's selling.
Wore my lucky sock for the big sale today. Never washed. Never fails.
Tried to lend a coin to a roommate and asked for it back the same day. They never return, do they?
# Off-game (the player): own lines, for a later [ooc] switch.
Sorry, missed that. Headset's only got one ear left, and it's the wrong one.

~ ding
Level {level}. My prices just went up with me.
Congratulations to me, of course. My rates will reflect the increased demand. One simply can't be too careful with profit.

~ grats
Grats, {0}! Now, what will you spend it on? I'm selling.

~ own_death
Dead. My purse is fine. My pride is not.
A lady should never be found sprawled like this. My purse is intact, at least.

~ other_death
{0}! Get up, you were carrying my share.
{0} fell? I'll guard your loot, naturally, for a modest consideration. It's pure profit, you see.
{0} fell. Up you get, and here, have this potion on the house. Just this once, you lucky duck.

~ drop
{item}! Sell it in town, or sell it to someone who doesn't know the price? Decisions.
Ah, the {item}! Pure profit. That sells, you know. I can name the merchant who'll snap it up.
{item}? That sells for almost nothing. One simply can't keep a full vault on such losses.

~ cheeky_reply banter [if tone_down off]
{0}, a lady doesn't answer that. A lady sends a bill.
{0}, really? That sounds like an absolute loss. How much did you waste on that foolishness?

# Midgard: Muckaduck, Norseman Runemaster, from Jordheim. Runes; she likes how the rune lines look, like a ledger.
=== midgard
~ ambient
Runes are a fine craft. I've carved a fortune from them. Not for you, though.
Another rune cut, another coin earned. I've lost count of both.
My coin pouch is the size of a Norse shield now. Hard to carry. Easy to love.
Jordheim has the finest market in Midgard. I know every stall. They know my prices.
Rune lines look just like a ledger, you know. Neat little columns. I cast my bolts, then I read them back, and every single one adds up to pure profit.
Somebody in Jordheim called me Jarl Muckaduck today. He was being sarcastic. I've decided not to know that, and I'm having it painted on a little sign anyway.
Every road in Midgard ends at a fjord. Deep, cold, dark water, all of it. I'd pay a dwarf a fortune to build me a bridge. Well. A modest sum. A sum.
Wore my lucky sock to the Jordheim market. Sold everything by noon. The stallholders kept their distance, but I put that down to respect.

~ opener realm
In Midgard the dwarves forge the best. I forge the best profit.
A Runemaster who crafts. Rich as a jarl, and twice as busy.
Barbalicious keeps inviting me round to see her house. Mine's in Jordheim, being decorated. It's been being decorated for a very long time. Rune by rune.

~ opener fun
I carved my own rune once. It meant: pay me. It worked.
Redtornader counts his vortices, I count my coins. We stood in Jordheim comparing numbers for an hour. I asked what his trophies would sell for. He hasn't spoken to me since.

~ opener trading
A troll in Jordheim paid me double for a boar tusk once. I didn't correct him. Rule eleven: when a boar is finally good for something, don't ask questions.

~ reply greeting
Hail, {0}! I'd carve you a hello, but I'm out of runes.

~ reply trading
A rune order, {0}? Out of stock. I'm always out of stock. It's very prestigious.

~ reply help
Crafting help, {0}? A Norse vow forbids it. I invented the vow.

~ ding
Level {level}! Is there a title that comes with this? Lady of Jordheim? No? Then what exactly have I been paying for?

~ own_death
Died. The runes should have warned me. I'll ask for a refund.
Ow. Dead in the snow, and a boar is standing on my purse. Of all the beasts to lose to. It isn't even going to drop anything worth selling.

~ drop
{item}! That'll fetch a pretty price in Jordheim. The stall by the... no, I'm not telling you which stall. Rule six.

# Albion: Muckaducke, Briton Wizard, from Ludlow. Fire and ice bolts; she wants a bigger house in Camelot City.
=== albion
~ ambient
Camelot has the finest guilds. I've paid every one of them. Twice.
Fire bolt, ice bolt, fire bolt. Hot, cold, hot. It's like the bath at home, only nobody's left the dishes in this one.
Llyn Barfog. Lovely name, lovely views, and a lake in the middle of it, very deep. One admires it from the road. One does not go in. One does not even look for too long.
Walked past the houses in Camelot again today and picked one out. Then I saw the price and picked a smaller one. Then a window box. I'll start with the window box.
I could tell you how rich I am, but the King might hear and ask for a loan.
Ludlow is lovely. Small, but lovely. Only until the manor in Camelot is ready.

~ opener realm
Camelot has titles the way Ludlow has boars. Sir this, Lady that. I asked at the palace whether they sell them. They gave me a very long look, and no price.
Camelot is lovely. I've bought a piece of it. A small piece. A very expensive piece.

~ opener fun
Shempenshire sold a perfectly good drop to the first merchant in Cotswold for three coppers. Three. I had to sit down. I bought it back for five and sold it for a fortune. Somebody has to.

~ opener trading
Rule twenty: a Ludlow price and a Camelot price are two different prices, and the difference is mine.

~ reply greeting
Good day, {0}! No orders today. I'm terribly sorry. I'm rich.

~ reply trading
A commission, {0}? The guild forbids it. I am the guild. It's a strict guild.

~ ding
{level}! Camelot will hear of this. I'll make sure it costs them.

~ own_death
Ow. Face down on the Salisbury Plains with my purse underneath me. Good. Nobody's getting at it. I'll just lie here and guard it, then.

~ drop
{item}! The vault has room. The vault always has room.

# Hibernia: Muckaduckeen, Elf Eldritch, from Tir na mBeo. Void and light nukes; Elves, she says, are born for business.
=== hibernia
~ ambient
Back home in Tir na mBeo they call me the richest Elf in town. They're right.
Void bolt, light bolt. Dark, bright, dark. Each one lands like a coin dropping into a pouch. I've started counting them as income. My accountant is me, and she agrees.
Slipped a potion to a young Lurikeen outside Ardee today. He thanked me. I told him I'd no idea what he was talking about, and I'd thank him not to tell anyone.
Hibernian markets glitter. Mine glitter and clink.

~ opener realm
Barbalicioush showed me round her house again. Lovely. Small. I told her mine in Tir na Nog is being decorated, in the Elven style. It takes centuries.
Tir na Nog has the finest vendors. I know all their prices. They hate it.

~ opener fun
Sunrayder asked me what his trophies would sell for, before I could ask him. Somebody who thinks like me. I've never felt so threatened.

~ opener trading
Hibernian prices are the most poetic. They rhyme with 'ouch'.

~ reply greeting
Good day, {0}. Muckaduckeen, with the "een". People forget the "een". People who forget the "een" pay full price.

~ reply trading
A commission, {0}? Booked until the next mist. Always the next mist.

~ ding
{level}! The fae approve. They've asked for a cut. I said no.

~ own_death
Ow. Killed by a boar in Connacht. A boar. An Elf of my standing. If anyone asks, it was a dragon, a very large one, and it dropped nothing because it was embarrassed.

~ drop
{item}! The mist brought me a gift. I'll sell it.
